== Avi Widgerson

=== Chapter 4 (continued)

Most problems are phrased as $x in L$ (string $x$ is in a language as covered in 240.) and a question with multiple outputs simply computes "is the first bit of output n" multiple times to get the full result. Reminds me of how $RR^n -> RR^m$ maps can be safely split into $m$ seperate $RR^n -> R$ functions.

Chapter 4 covers CSPs and Unique Games Conjecture (which was recently proven true by OpenAI).

Every csp problem has a hard proportion $p$ such that solving less than or equal to $p$ is $P$. And above $p$ is $N P$-hard.

Unique Games. The simple phrasing is in terms of linear equations. But an interesting concept is _interactive proof systems_ where an unlimited computational capacity prover is trying to convince a limited computational power verifier that a statement is true.

This is similar to NP class where task is to produce a polynomial size proof. These prover-verifier setups get more interesting if you have 2 provers, and a verifier with access to randomness. This turns the setup into a sort of zero-knowledge proof game type thing. (hence unique games name).

Next Avi covers distP which is average case running time on distributions. Apparently randomness screws with many things, especially with weird distributions. The solution is a "efficiently samplable distribution" which is a distribution that can be efficiently generated by an algorithm with access to a coin.

This is a rather creative way to define things, and I will keep this idea of "nice inputs are those which can be easily created on purpose" it sounds like it would be useful.

Another interesting assumption is "nature does not perform inefficient computations" which is used to restrict the sorts of problems that arise in practice.

The chapter finishes with a primer on cryptography, most of which I already know to some extent. (one way hash functions, modular arithmetic for diffe-hellman).

Noteable functions are that factoring integers is hard, and that taking logarithm in modular arithmetic is hard.

=== Chapter 5

Cantor diagonalization style proofs are useful for proving lower bounds and complexity. (And non-existance of fast algorithms.) Halting problem was proven this way, Godel's stuff apparently too.

There is some sort of DTIME($n^2) != $ DTIME($n^3$) thing called Time Hierarchy Theorem. But I didn't really understand it, even after discussing it with LLMs.

Figured out whats happening. An _oracle_ (e.g. $A$) is a component of a turing machine that can answer a specific question for cheap. From here, the main turing machine can be seen as reducing the problem it is solving to the oracle.

The oracle is useful for proving that problems reduce to each other in polynomial time, even if we don't know the complexity class of the problem we are reducing to.

Apparently in 1975 this was used to show that diagonalization technique won't solve P vs. NP, because there exist oracles such that $P=N P$ and $P!=N P$. This is called _relativization_, which I don't quite understand yet, but the idea seems to be that most proof techniques require "forall" statements. So if it turns out we can't guarantee a "forall" to the proof technique, we know the problem is trickier.

_algebraization_ is some kind of generalization of _relativization_. Avi Widgerson showed that it is unhelpful to P vs. NP.

==== 5.2 Boolean Circuits

I know a lot of basics from elsewhere.

Circuits are strange because they allow parallelization, and also have fixed input sizes (_nonuniformity_). This makes circuit complexity be the size of the circuit in terms of the input size. There is a lot of strength (and hard proofs) here because the circuits for different input sizes of the same problem don't need to follow a common construction pattern. Proving lower bounds for circuits is hard.

Apparently there are functions that are undcidable for Turing Machines (e.g. Diophantine equations), but have linear boolean circuits. This is possible because Turing Machines need the same algorithm for any input size, whereas boolean circuits can construct a new circuit for every size. Though I am curious how they formalized this construction while making it impossible to write an algorithm that constructs the boolean circuit and then simulates it. "Smallest number that can't be written in less than 100 characters." sort of situation it seems.

$P / p o l y$ class is called polynomial with "advice" where the turing machine, besides the input, also gets an encoded boolean circuit (advice on how to compute the function). Apparently this class contain all of $P$ but we don't know if it contains $N P$ (if we knew, P vs. NP would be solved).

(I already heard this in CSC240 tutorial) most boolean functions need exponential size circuits, because there are just way more functions ($2^2^n$) than there are small circuits. Indeed we have a "smallest number that can't be written in less than 100 characters." problem.

There is clearly a lot of open problems in boolean circuit complexity. But this sort of hides all the progress that was made, and it is likely that currently all everyone cares about are the limited set of incredibly difficult open problems from which everything else trivially follows.

== Other Stuff

=== The 3 Models of Computation

There are apparently 3 seperate models of computation which all act slightly differently.

_Boolean circuits_ with fixed input that focus only on depth. (Massive parallelizm is allowed). Computational classes are $A C$, $N C$ etc. People mess with these by adding different special gates and proving things with these new special gates added.

_Turing Machine_ which notably has an $O(n)$ array indexing speed. (and takes an unlimited size word $x$ as input). This has interesting consequences like time-memory of algorithms being related. For example PSPACE class contains NP space etc. algorithms.

_RAM model_ what we know as normal computer with fast array indexing.

It would appear that our computers differ from these mathematical models. Certain operations are clearly faster, others are slower than some of these models.
